WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.437
The federal requirement.

2
00:00:01.437 --> 00:00:06.195
A package is the server baseline in
a file, so what was built, from what,

3
00:00:06.195 --> 00:00:11.631
and what shipped is the evidence for
NIST special publication eight hundred

4
00:00:11.631 --> 00:00:15.963
fifty-three controls such as S R
three, S A ten, S I seven and C M two.

5
00:00:15.963 --> 00:00:18.343
The pinned module versions support supply

6
00:00:18.343 --> 00:00:20.988
chain and developer
configuration management.

7
00:00:20.988 --> 00:00:25.888
The package check, refusing build-host
data, supports developer testing.

8
00:00:25.888 --> 00:00:28.890
The same commit giving
the same cleaned MOF hash,

9
00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:33.396
with the hashes identifying what
shipped, supports software integrity

10
00:00:33.396 --> 00:00:37.225
and the baseline, and the provenance
manifest is the evidence.

11
00:00:37.225 --> 00:00:40.397
The definition carries the
package's content hash,

12
00:00:40.397 --> 00:00:42.587
and Arc servers are a stated choice.

13
00:00:42.587 --> 00:00:46.748
A version that didn't rise, a mode
in the wrong case and an enforcing

14
00:00:46.748 --> 00:00:51.525
definition with no owner filter are
refused, in the pull request's check run.

15
00:00:51.525 --> 00:00:54.861
And Lab 2 adds the change
record for the published blob,

16
00:00:54.861 --> 00:00:56.788
and the role assignment record.

17
00:00:56.788 --> 00:01:01.721
The Key Security Indicators come from
FedRAMP's Consolidated Rules for 2026:

18
00:01:01.721 --> 00:01:04.959
they're FedRAMP 20x
indicators for providers,

19
00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:07.888
and the agency lines its
evidence up with them.

20
00:01:07.888 --> 00:01:11.520
Which output supports which
control is this workbook's reading.
